The loop is the change that pays on every size. Preallocating `min(first.length, set.size)` ties the loop on the 50% overlap bench, so it is not worth it.

Stopping when the set is empty is result-identical, and it is the large win when the second array is small and its keys show up early. On the repo’s 50% overlap bench it never fires: 5,000 keys are still in the set at the end. It also stops calling `mapper` on the rest of the first array. The implementation today calls `mapper` for every first-array element, including when the second array is empty.

Indexing the second array is the right side to hash. Output order and element identity come from the first array, and each matched key has to be consumed.

